                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Cells are microscopic structures that cannot be seen with the naked eye. They require magnification to be visible.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the definition of a microscope?\n2. How does a microscope work?\n3. What are the different types of microscopes?\n4. Which type of microscope is best for viewing plant cells?\n\nAnswers:\n1. A microscope is an instrument used to magnify and observe small objects or organisms that cannot be seen with the naked eye.\n2. A microscope works by using lenses to magnify the image of an object or organism. Light microscopes use visible light to illuminate the specimen, while electron microscopes use a beam of electrons.\n3. The two main types of microscopes are light microscopes and electron microscopes. Light microscopes are used to view living or non-living specimens, while electron microscopes are used to view extremely small specimens at high magnification.\n4. The best type of microscope for viewing plant cells is a light microscope, specifically a compound microscope. This type of microscope uses two lenses to magnify the image and can provide a clear view of the cells in an oak tree leaf. Therefore, the best tool for Jessica to use to see the cells in an oak tree leaf is a microscope (option B).",
