                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question: In which aspect are protons and electrons always equal?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step:\n\nA) Mass number: Protons and electrons have different masses. The mass of a proton is approximately 1 atomic mass unit (amu), while the mass of an electron is much smaller, approximately 1/1836 amu. So, they are not equal in mass number.\n\nB) Kinetic energy: The kinetic energy of a particle depends on its mass and velocity. Since protons and electrons have different masses and can have different velocities, their kinetic energies are not always equal.\n\nC) Sign of charge: Protons have a positive charge, while electrons have a negative charge. So, they have opposite signs of charge, not equal.\n\nD) Magnitude of charge: Protons have a charge of +1 elementary charge, while electrons have a charge of -1 elementary charge. The magnitudes of their charges are equal, both being 1 elementary charge.\n\nBased on our analysis, the correct answer is D) Magnitude of charge. Protons and electrons are always equal in the magnitude of their charge.",
