                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is A) Heat from the room is absorbed by the ice cube.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\nWhen an ice cube is set out in a warm room, it starts to melt and eventually turns into liquid water. This happens because of the transfer of heat from the room to the ice cube.\n\nHeat always flows from a warmer object to a cooler object until they reach the same temperature. In this case, the warm air in the room has a higher temperature than the ice cube, which is at 0\u00b0C (32\u00b0F). Therefore, heat flows from the room to the ice cube.\n\nAs the ice cube absorbs heat from the room, its temperature starts to rise. When the temperature of the ice cube reaches 0\u00b0C, the heat energy starts to break the bonds between the water molecules in the ice, causing it to melt.\n\nThe process of melting continues until all the ice has turned into liquid water. At this point, the temperature of the water is the same as the temperature of the room, and there is no more heat transfer between them.\n\nTherefore, the correct statement that describes the reason why an ice cube melts when set out in a warm room is A) Heat from the room is absorbed by the ice cube.",
