                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm not sure which of these is a chemical property of a material. Can you help me understand the difference between physical and chemical properties?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Physical properties are characteristics that can be observed or measured without changing the composition of the material. Chemical properties, on the other hand, describe how a substance reacts with other substances, which results in a change in the composition of the material.\n\nStudent: Okay, so let's go through the options one by one. Is density a chemical property?\n\nProfessor: Density is a physical property because it can be measured without changing the composition of the material. It is the mass of a substance per unit volume.\n\nStudent: What about flammability? Is that a chemical property?\n\nProfessor: Yes, flammability is a chemical property because it describes how a substance reacts with oxygen in the air to produce heat and light. This reaction, called combustion, results in a change in the composition of the material.\n\nStudent: I see. And what about color? Is that a chemical property?\n\nProfessor: Color is a physical property because it can be observed without changing the composition of the material. It is the result of the way a substance reflects or absorbs light.\n\nStudent: Lastly, is brittleness a chemical property?\n\nProfessor: Brittle materials break easily under stress, which is a physical property. It can be observed without changing the composition of the material.\n\nStudent: So, based on our discussion, the correct answer is B) flammability, since it's the only chemical property among the options.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! Well done. Do you have any other questions about chemical properties?\n\nStudent: No, I think I understand now. Thank you for your help, Professor!\n\nProfessor: You're welcome! Don't hesitate to ask if you have any more questions in the future.",
