                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nA) density is a chemical property of a material.\n\nChemical properties are characteristics of a material that describe its ability to undergo a chemical change or reaction when it interacts with other materials. Examples of chemical properties include flammability, reactivity, and acidity.\n\nDensity is a measure of the mass of a material per unit of volume. It is a physical property, but it can also be used to infer the chemical composition of a material. For example, a material with a high density is likely to be composed of heavier elements than a material with a low density. Therefore, density is a chemical property of a material.\n\nB) flammability is a chemical property of a material.\n\nFlammability is the ability of a material to burn or ignite when exposed to an open flame or other source of ignition. It is a chemical property because it is related to the chemical composition of the material. For example, materials that contain a high amount of hydrocarbons are more likely to be flammable than materials that contain fewer hydrocarbons. Therefore, flammability is a chemical property of a material.\n\nC) color is not a chemical property of a material.\n\nColor is a physical property of a material that describes the visible light reflected off of its surface. It is not a chemical property because it does not describe the material's ability to undergo a chemical change or reaction when it interacts with other materials. Therefore, color is not a chemical property of a material.\n\nD) brittleness is not a chemical property of a material.\n\nBrittleness is a physical property of a material that describes its ability to break or shatter when subjected to a force. It is not a chemical property because it does not describe the material's ability to undergo a chemical change or reaction when it interacts with other materials. Therefore, brittleness is not a chemical property of a material.",
