                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Water can exist in three states of matter: solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor). The state of matter of a substance can change when it gains or loses energy (heat). \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What happens to the temperature of water when it freezes?\n2. What happens to the temperature of ice when it melts?\n3. What happens to the mass of water when it freezes?\n4. What happens to the mass of ice when it melts?\n\nAnswers:\n1. When water freezes, its temperature decreases as it loses energy and transitions from a liquid to a solid state.\n2. When ice melts, its temperature increases as it gains energy and transitions from a solid to a liquid state.\n3. When water freezes, its mass stays the same.\n4. When ice melts, its mass stays the same.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the original question is B) Both samples in the cups will be in the same state. The group that froze water will have a cup of ice, and the group that melted ice will have a cup of water. The temperature and mass of the cups may be different, but both samples will be in different states of matter.",
